<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Silves</h1><p class="st-meta">1027 CE – 1063 CE (approx.) · capital Silves</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A taifa in the western Algarve ruled by the Banu Muzayn from Silves, then a rich river port and later a city of poets under Seville.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1027 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-muzayn">Muhammad ibn Muzayn</a>, from a local Arab family, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arade">Arade</a> river. The town had grown into the chief city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, and the family ruled it as an independent principality amid the confusion following the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a>, with its red sandstone castle and navigable river, traded in figs, dried fruit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and was known for its pure Arabic speech and poetry. The Banu Muzayn ruled a small territory and had to fend off the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a> and the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-santa-maria-del-algarve">Taifa of Santa María del Algarve</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1063 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid-of-seville">al-Mutadid of Seville</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> and made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">al-Mutamid</a> governor, with the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-ammar">Ibn Ammar</a> at his side. The city later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-silves">Second Taifa of Silves</a> in 1144.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-silves.jpg" alt="Taifa of Silves" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Silves.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1027 CE – 1063 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Silves</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Muzayn, Isa ibn Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Muzayn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1045&amp;focus=taifa-of-silves" aria-label="Taifa of Silves on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4469 839 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Silves lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4789 1095L4850 1088"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4789 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4789 1095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Silves. <a href="/atlas?year=1045&amp;focus=taifa-of-silves">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

